      <h1>Questions</h1>
      <p class="lede">The ten we are actually asked, answered at the length the
        answer deserves.</p>
      <h2>Before you book</h2>
      <div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need to be experienced?</summary><p>You need to have practised before &mdash; a few months of regular classes is plenty. Two hours a morning is a lot if the postures are entirely new, and you would spend the week learning vocabulary instead of practising. Rishikesh is the exception in the other direction: three hours before breakfast is demanding and most people who book it have been on one of the others first.</p></details><details><summary>Can I come alone?</summary><p>Most people do. Between two thirds and three quarters of every group books alone, and the group size is capped partly so that nobody spends a week unspoken to. Sharing a room with a stranger is standard in Goa and Bali and we match people carefully; if you would rather not, a single room is $280 or $340.</p></details><details><summary>Can my partner come without practising?</summary><p>In Goa, easily &mdash; there is a beach, eleven places to eat and nothing to be late for. In Bali it works less well because it is remote and there is a second session in the afternoon. In Rishikesh we would gently talk you out of it: it is dry, vegetarian, up a hill, and the week is built entirely around the practice.</p></details><details><summary>What if I am injured?</summary><p>Tell us when you book, in detail, and tell Tess again on the first morning. Most things are worked around. A few &mdash; a recent operation, an unstable shoulder, an acute disc problem &mdash; mean a very reduced practice, and it is better to know that in advance than to find out on a roof in Goa.</p></details><details><summary>Is the food really all vegetarian?</summary><p>In Goa and Rishikesh, yes. In Bali there is fish twice a week for anybody who wants it. We cater for vegan, coeliac, nut allergy and most other requirements if you tell us at least four weeks ahead; in Rishikesh the kitchen also cooks without onion or garlic as standard, which occasionally surprises people.</p></details><details><summary>How fit do I need to be?</summary><p>Fit enough for forty-one steps up to the guesthouse in Rishikesh and a three-hour walk if you want the waterfall. Bali has steps to most bungalows. Goa is flat. None of the practice requires strength you do not already have.</p></details><details><summary>What about single occupancy?</summary><p>Rishikesh is single as standard at no extra cost. Goa is $280 and Bali is $340, which is close to what the empty second bed costs us. We do not profit from single travellers and we do not charge a percentage.</p></details><details><summary>Do you run teacher training?</summary><p>No, and we do not intend to. There is no certificate at the end of any of these weeks and no hours that count towards anything.</p></details><details><summary>Is there wifi?</summary><p>Yes in all three, and it is fine in Goa, patchy in Bali and slow in Rishikesh. We do not collect phones, ban devices or lecture anybody about digital detox. You are an adult on holiday.</p></details><details><summary>What happens if a retreat does not fill?</summary><p>It runs anyway. We have run Rishikesh with six people and Bali with nine, and the price does not change. We have never cancelled a retreat for low numbers and would not.</p></details></div>
    </div>
